<PageShell page={page} h1="Snagging in Dubai: what it costs and what it catches" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2>What does snagging cost in Dubai?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Published snagging prices in Dubai by unit type"
    columns={['Unit', 'Price', 'Notes']}
    rows={[
      [
        'Typical apartment',
        'AED 666–999',
        'The range most published Dubai rate cards fall inside',
      ],
      [
        'Studio',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: published studio rate]</span>',
        'Usually below the apartment rate',
      ],
      [
        'Villa or townhouse',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: published villa rate by bedroom count]</span>',
        'Priced by size and system count',
      ],
      [
        'Re-inspection',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: published re-inspection fee]</span>',
        'Charged separately by most firms, and rarely advertised',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Published Dubai snagging rate cards"
  />

  <p>
    The full breakdown by unit size, including the re-inspection fee most firms do not put on
    their website, is on <a href="/snagging-dubai/cost/">snagging costs in Dubai</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Against a property worth AED 1.3–3.5 million, the inspection is a rounding error. That is
    not an argument for buying one — it is an argument for not choosing an inspector on price.
    The number that matters is what the survey finds and whether the report is detailed enough
    to make the developer act on it.
  </p>

  <h2>What does a snagging inspection actually cover?</h2>

  <p>
    A survey worth paying for is instrumented, not visual. The categories below are what
    separates an inspection from a careful walkthrough.
  </p>

  <ul class="two-col">
    <li>Mechanical, electrical and plumbing, tested rather than looked at</li>
    <li>Air-conditioning output measured at each outlet</li>
    <li>Water pressure and drainage falls, including balconies and wet areas</li>
    <li>Thermal imaging for concealed leaks and insulation gaps</li>
    <li>Electrical socket polarity, earthing and distribution board labelling</li>
    <li>Joinery, alignment, and door and window operation under load</li>
    <li>Waterproofing in bathrooms, kitchens and terraces</li>
    <li>Glazing, sealant lines and external finishes</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The complete room-by-room list is set out on{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/checklist/">the Dubai snagging checklist</a>, which runs to 180
    points and is free to download.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the developer obliged to fix?</h2>

  <p>
    Defects for one year and structural failure for ten, under the liability a developer
    carries in the UAE. The one-year window is the one that expires while people are still
    unpacking. The governing text belongs on this page quoted verbatim with its article number
    rather than summarised:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="UAE Civil Code articles on decennial liability and defect liability, verbatim with article numbers" />
  </p>

  <p>
    What that window means in practice, and what happens if you miss it, is covered on{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/developer-defect-liability/">developer defect liability in Dubai</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What do people get wrong about snagging?</h2>

  <p>
    They treat the report as the end of the process. It is the beginning. The sequence that
    actually works is: inspect before accepting handover, issue the report to the developer,
    let them complete the work, then <em>re-inspect</em> and only then accept. The re-inspection
    is where the value is, because the first round of fixes is frequently partial and nobody
    checks.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second common error is sequencing snagging after utility activation and move-in. Once
    you have accepted the unit, taken the keys and started living in it, the argument about
    whether a defect was there at handover becomes yours to prove. The full order of
    operations is on <a href="/handover-dubai/">the Dubai handover checklist</a>.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If the snag list comes back worse than you expected and you are reconsidering the purchase altogether, "
    anchor="a current valuation of the unit"
    after=" is the first number worth having before you decide anything else."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
